1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,928
<font color="#000000">Can large language models combine skills they
already know?</font>

2
00:00:05,008 --> 00:00:13,792
<font color="#000000">This COLM 2024 paper tests whether models solve an
unseen composite task by fusing two simple tasks seen separately.</font>

3
00:00:13,872 --> 00:00:17,460
<font color="#000000">Across Llama and GPT, a sharp dichotomy emerges.</font>

4
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:21,725
<font color="#000000">Suppose a model learned two simple tasks in-context,</font>

5
00:00:21,805 --> 00:00:25,308
<font color="#000000">capitalizing certain words and swapping others.</font>

6
00:00:25,388 --> 00:00:29,502
<font color="#000000">Given an input needing both, can it combine the skills?</font>

7
00:00:29,582 --> 00:00:34,610
<font color="#000000">For humans this is trivial, yet even GPT-4 and
Claude 3 often fail.</font>

8
00:00:34,990 --> 00:00:39,042
<font color="#000000">Models are increasingly asked to chain skills for
real reasoning,</font>

9
00:00:39,122 --> 00:00:42,284
<font color="#000000">yet we lack a clear account of when chaining works.</font>

10
00:00:42,364 --> 00:00:45,081
<font color="#000000">Prior studies are narrow with little theory,</font>

11
00:00:45,161 --> 00:00:48,450
<font color="#000000">and the failure reproduces easily on frontier models.</font>

12
00:00:48,830 --> 00:00:55,795
<font color="#000000">Three contributions: a test suite of linguistic and
logical composite tasks with simple-task examples only;</font>

13
00:00:55,875 --> 00:01:00,206
<font color="#000000">evaluation across Llama and GPT scales revealing a
clear dichotomy;</font>

14
00:01:00,286 --> 00:01:04,880
<font color="#000000">and a linear self-attention theory explaining when
composition emerges.</font>

15
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:08,632
<font color="#000000">Two halves.</font>

16
00:01:08,712 --> 00:01:12,083
<font color="#000000">Empirically,</font>

17
00:01:12,163 --> 00:01:15,535
<font color="#000000">each composite task is tested in four settings with
ten in-context examples: each simple task alone,</font>

18
00:01:15,615 --> 00:01:18,987
<font color="#000000">a composite test with simple-task demos, and an
all-composite gold standard.</font>

19
00:01:19,067 --> 00:01:22,438
<font color="#000000">Theoretically,</font>

20
00:01:22,518 --> 00:01:25,890
<font color="#000000">a linear self-attention model shows composition
succeeds under confined support.</font>

21
00:01:26,270 --> 00:01:28,975
<font color="#000000">The suite pairs simple building blocks.</font>

22
00:01:29,055 --> 00:01:32,546
<font color="#000000">Linguistic tasks include capitalization, swapping,</font>

23
00:01:32,626 --> 00:01:37,189
<font color="#000000">and translations like phrase recombination and
passive-to-active.</font>

24
00:01:37,269 --> 00:01:41,045
<font color="#000000">Logical tasks combine arithmetic with word operations.</font>

25
00:01:41,125 --> 00:01:46,116
<font color="#000000">Tasks split into separable composites, acting on
different input parts,</font>

26
00:01:46,196 --> 00:01:50,330
<font color="#000000">and compose-by-step composites requiring chained
reasoning.</font>

27
00:01:50,710 --> 00:01:54,474
<font color="#000000">A sharp split.</font>

28
00:01:54,554 --> 00:01:58,318
<font color="#000000">On separable composite tasks, models compose well
and improve with scale,</font>

29
00:01:58,398 --> 00:02:02,162
<font color="#000000">approaching the gold standard.</font>

30
00:02:02,242 --> 00:02:06,006
<font color="#000000">On compose-by-step tasks they collapse: Llama
solves each simple task near ninety percent,</font>

31
00:02:06,086 --> 00:02:09,850
<font color="#000000">but the composite drops below twenty percent, and
scaling doesn't help.</font>

32
00:02:10,230 --> 00:02:12,785
<font color="#000000">The scale sweep is the key ablation.</font>

33
00:02:12,865 --> 00:02:16,079
<font color="#000000">On separable tasks accuracy rises with scale;</font>

34
00:02:16,159 --> 00:02:19,813
<font color="#000000">on compose-by-step tasks it stays flat or degrades.</font>

35
00:02:19,893 --> 00:02:24,717
<font color="#000000">Swapping simple-task demos for composite ones
recovers performance,</font>

36
00:02:24,797 --> 00:02:28,890
<font color="#000000">isolating composition, not capability, as the bottleneck.</font>

37
00:02:29,270 --> 00:02:30,658
<font color="#000000">The numbers tell it.</font>

38
00:02:30,738 --> 00:02:35,651
<font color="#000000">Simple capitalization and swap reach about ninety
percent for Llama.</font>

39
00:02:35,731 --> 00:02:40,129
<font color="#000000">The compose-by-step version falls to twenty percent
or lower,</font>

40
00:02:40,209 --> 00:02:42,038
<font color="#000000">with no gain from scaling.</font>

41
00:02:42,118 --> 00:02:46,810
<font color="#000000">Milder separable cases climb to forty-four and
sixty-six percent.</font>

42
00:02:47,190 --> 00:02:53,444
<font color="#000000">The takeaway: models combine two skills only when
they act on separate parts of the input.</font>

43
00:02:53,524 --> 00:02:58,301
<font color="#000000">When a task needs genuinely chained, multi-step
reasoning, they fail,</font>

44
00:02:58,381 --> 00:03:00,482
<font color="#000000">and more parameters won't help.</font>

45
00:03:00,562 --> 00:03:03,720
<font color="#000000">Task structure predicts whether scaling helps.</font>
